      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A=\\{1,3,5,7\\}.$ \n\n$$\nR=\\{(1,3),(1,5),(3,5),(5,7)\\}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nWe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1,1){{field-1}}R,\\,(3,3){{field-2}}R,\\,(5,5){{field-3}}R,\\,(7,7){{field-4}}R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n{{field-5}}{{field-6}}, \n\nthe relation is \n{{field-7}}.",
      "worked_solution": "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nIn our case, the relation is defined on $\\{1,3,5,7\\},$ and we have\n\n$$\n(1,1)\\notin R,\\,(3,3)\\notin R,\\,(5,5)\\notin R,\\,(7,7)\\notin R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\n$\\,$ Since $(x,x)\\notin R$ $\\text{for all }x\\in A,$ the relation is $\\text{irreflexive}.$",
